    {
      "short": "DNS",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "title": "First, find the address",
      "body": [
        "Computers connect to IP addresses, not names. So the browser first asks a <span class=\"term\">DNS resolver</span> — usually run by the ISP or a public DNS service — to turn <code>plotline.app</code> into a number like <code>203.0.113.7</code>.",
        "If the resolver hasn't seen the name recently, it asks the domain's <span class=\"term\">authoritative name server</span> and caches the reply for as long as the record's TTL allows. Both servers are near Ines, so this costs about <b>30 ms</b>."
      ],
      "callout": {
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Answers are cached at every layer — browser, operating system, resolver. On a repeat visit this step usually costs nothing."
      },
      "changes": [
        "+ DNS resolver at Ines's ISP",
        "+ Authoritative name server for plotline.app",
        "~ Laptop now knows 203.0.113.7"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "dns": 30,
        "connect": null,
        "request": null,
        "download": null,
        "total": 30
